    "turns": [
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "The previous story examined AlphaTensor. Now the camera turns to SayCan and a new question along the same timeline: If we take SayCan apart, which idea truly changed the direction of research?",
            "sourceIds": [],
            "claimIds": [],
            "contentOrigin": "storyline-sequence"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Large language models could propose plausible high-level plans, but they did not know which actions a particular robot could execute in its current environment. Robotics policies knew local skills, yet lacked the language knowledge needed to interpret open-ended instructions.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-saycan-paper", "source-primary-record"],
            "claimIds": [
                "claim-annual-membership",
                "claim-publication-record",
                "claim-contributor-record",
                "claim-core-method",
                "claim-historical-significance"
            ],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.historical-background"
        },
        {
            "role": "A",
            "text": "That sounds like a promising direction. What mechanism actually makes it work?",
            "sourceIds": [],
            "claimIds": [],
            "contentOrigin": "editorial"
        },
        {
            "role": "B",
            "text": "SayCan scores each candidate skill twice: the language model estimates whether it advances the instruction, and a value function estimates whether the robot can perform it now. Their product selects the next skill, and the process repeats after the environment changes.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-saycan-paper", "source-primary-record"],
            "claimIds": [
                "claim-annual-membership",
                "claim-publication-record",
                "claim-contributor-record",
                "claim-core-method",
                "claim-historical-significance"
            ],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.core-idea"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Experts generally treat SayCan as an early and influential blueprint for combining foundation models with embodied control rather than letting language models act as unconstrained planners. Its usefulness-feasibility split reappears in later vision-language-action and robotic agent systems.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-saycan-paper", "source-primary-record"],
            "claimIds": [
                "claim-annual-membership",
                "claim-publication-record",
                "claim-contributor-record",
                "claim-core-method",
                "claim-historical-significance"
            ],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.long-term-legacy"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Seen alongside AlphaTensor, SayCan reveals how the same technical question can echo across different eras.",
            "sourceIds": [
